Cramp or Spasm (Undifferentiated Finding)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Sudden painful involuntary contraction, Palpable muscle knot, Relief via passive stretch

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, unrelenting, excruciating lower limb muscle contraction rendering the patient immobile and highly distressed.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden onset of agonizing leg pain waking the patient from sleep, with a visible, rock-hard muscle deformity and total inability to walk. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to rapidly differentiate a benign severe muscle spasm from an acute deep vein thrombosis or acute limb ischemia, and to provide immediate mechanical and pharmacological relief prior to the morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My calf muscle suddenly tied itself into a rock-hard knot while I was sleeping, and the pain is blinding."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe episode of right calf muscle contraction. Timeline: Patient went to bed well following an unusually long day of standing on hard surfaces at work; abruptly awakened two hours ago by a sudden, involuntary, and exceedingly painful contraction of the right gastrocnemius muscle. The acute contraction lasted 15 minutes, leaving a profound, deep aching soreness. However, minor twitching persists, and the patient is terrified to move the leg for fear of triggering another massive spasm, rendering them unable to walk to the bathroom.
Functional Status: Temporarily homebound, refusing to weight-bear on the affected limb.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to sudden leg weakness and fear-avoidant gait.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/90 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, diaphoretic, guarding the right lower limb.
Physical Exam: Right lower limb reveals a visibly taut right gastrocnemius muscle belly. Palpation demonstrates a residual, hard, knot-like mass in the medial head of the gastrocnemius with focal tenderness. Passive dorsiflexion of the ankle (stretching the calf) initially elicits guarding but ultimately provides visible relief of residual twitching. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are strong and symmetric. Capillary refill is <2 seconds. No unilateral pitting edema or warmth. Sensation intact globally.
Environmental Observations: Patient is dehydrated; empty water glass on the nightstand, reports poor fluid intake during the long work shift.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Acute Muscle Cramp (Gastrocnemius Spasm) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out clinically by the sudden, instantaneous onset (seconds) waking the patient, palpable taut muscular band, absence of diffuse calf swelling, and negative Homan's sign.

Acute Limb Ischemia - Ruled out by the presence of strong, bounding distal pulses, normal skin temperature, and brisk capillary refill.

Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out by intact plantarflexion strength, absence of a palpable gap in the tendon, and negative Thompson squeeze test.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic, severe nocturnal muscle cramp, characterized by sudden, agonizing pain, a palpable muscle knot, and partial relief with passive stretching. The acute distress and absolute refusal to weight-bear necessitated an urgent after-hours visit to exclude limb-threatening vascular events like DVT or acute ischemia. Clinical examination definitively established the musculoskeletal nature of the pathology and normal vascular status. Postponing evaluation would have resulted in unmanaged severe anxiety, sleep deprivation, and an unsafe functional state. The home visit safely broke the spasm cycle, provided reassurance, and established hydration and mechanical strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short 3-day course of oral Diazepam 2mg Nocté PRN if severe spasms recur to allow sleep. Recommended oral rehydration salts for 24 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Keep a towel next to the bed; if a spasm starts, loop the towel around the ball of the foot and gently pull the toes toward the knee. Consequence: Immediate mechanical lengthening of the cramping muscle fibers, physically forcing the spasm to break and stopping the severe pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Drink a 500ml glass of electrolyte water 30 minutes before going to sleep. Consequence: Restoration of intramuscular cellular balance, drastically reducing the likelihood of the muscle misfiring during the night (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the cramps become a frequent, nightly occurrence to screen for metabolic or vascular causes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the leg becomes continuously swollen, hot, red, or if the foot turns completely white, cold, and numb.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of a benign muscle spasm. Recurrent cramps require routine blood tests to check kidney and thyroid function. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute knot has released, but the muscle will feel deeply bruised and sore for 24-48 hours, much like having completed a heavy workout.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if your entire lower leg swells up rapidly, if you develop a fever, or if you lose feeling in your toes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A cramp is an electrical misfire causing the muscle to lock in its shortest position. Passive stretching physically pulls those locked fibers apart. Staying hydrated ensures the electrical signals in the muscle behave normally.
Actionable Steps: Use the towel stretch immediately if you feel a twitch starting. Do not point your toes down while sleeping, as this puts the calf in the shortened position where it is most likely to cramp.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe calf muscle cramp (spasm). Checked pulses, blood flow is normal. Stay hydrated, use the towel stretch, and take prescribed muscle relaxant if needed.

